    The infimum of the individual ratios $r_1(q)\coloneqq  \frac{\mathcal{F}_{\rho_1}^{f_q}}{\mathcal{F}_{\sigma}^{f_q}}$ and $r_2(q)\coloneqq \frac{\mathcal{F}_{\rho_2}^{f_q}}{\mathcal{F}_{\sigma}^{f_q}}$ are $1/4$ and $0$, which equal the conversion rate of $R(\rho_1\to\sigma)$ and $R(\rho_2\to\sigma)$, respectively. The sum, $r_1(q)+r_2(q)$ are $1/2$ for all $q\in[1/2,1)$, hence the joint conversion rate $R(\rho_1\otimes\rho_2\to\sigma)=1/2$. Thus, the zero-rate mixed state $\rho_2$ doubles the resulting yield when jointly processed with $\rho_1$.} 
